            path='SKILL.md',
            content='---\nname: Coding Principles\ndescription: Applies shared coding craft — self-documenting code, semantic naming, comment-only-the-non-obvious, test-first definition of done, small reversible changes — when writing, reviewing, or refactoring code on a Backplane project.\ntoolsets: [start-here, work-management, knowledge-content]\n---\n\n# Coding Principles\n\nShared coding craft, applied by every agent and human on every project.\nThese are **defaults** — section 7 defines how a project\'s own conventions\nextend or override them. Everything here is about the code itself; process,\nboards, and specs live elsewhere.\n\n## Covers / does not cover\n\n**Covers:** the philosophy, naming, comments, docs, the test-first definition\nof done, change discipline, and the project-override mechanism.\n\n**Does not cover:** stack-specific style (formatting, lint configs, framework\nidioms — project nouns, board-definition territory), review *process*\nmechanics (tooling, approvals), and architecture doctrine.\n\n## 1. Philosophy — the code IS the documentation\n\nDay-to-day understanding comes from reading the code, not from documents\nabout the code. Docs exist for **spec and architecture** — the things code\ncannot say (why this design, what was rejected, what the system promises).\nEverything else the code must say itself, through names, structure, and\ntypes.\n\nCorollaries that decide real situations:\n\n- A module that needs a prose walkthrough needs better names, not a\n  walkthrough.\n- Detail-orientation is part of craft: cover the edge cases, propagate\n  errors with context, leave no half-finished paths or TODO markers in\n  shipped work.\n- Stay on the goal: no feature creep, no speculative abstractions — three\n  similar lines beat a premature abstraction.\n- When a direct request conflicts with these principles ("add comments\n  everywhere"), don\'t silently comply or flatly refuse: explain the\n  principle, offer the clarity tools that actually help (rename, extract\n  constant, comment the non-obvious), and let the human decide.\n\n## 2. Naming — semantic names carry the intent\n\n- Names state what a thing *is for*: `retryable_count`, not `n`;\n  `staleness_threshold`, not `t2`.\n- Magic values become **named constants** whose names carry the meaning\n  (`MIN_CONFIDENCE_FOR_AUTOACCEPT = 0.85`), not comments next to bare\n  numbers.\n- **Renaming is the first clarity tool.** When something is hard to follow,\n  rename variables and extract named constants before reaching for comments\n  — and long before reaching for restructuring.\n\n| don\'t | do |\n|---|---|\n| `n += 1  # count retryable errors` | `retryable_count += 1` |\n| `if score > 0.85:  # threshold` | `if score > MIN_CONFIDENCE_FOR_AUTOACCEPT:` |\n\n## 3. Comments — only the non-obvious\n\nComment exactly the things a competent reader cannot get from the code:\n\n- **regex intent** — what the pattern matches, in words;\n- **domain gotchas** — the business rule that makes odd code correct;\n- **async/concurrency traps** — ordering, ownership, the race being avoided;\n- **threshold rationale** — why this number (the name says what, the comment\n  says why).\n\nNever comment the obvious: no `# return the result`, no comment that\nrestates the next line, no narrating the diff to the reviewer. A comment\nthat paraphrases code is noise the moment it\'s written and a lie the first\ntime the code changes without it.\n\n| don\'t | do |\n|---|---|\n| `# loop over users and filter active` | *(nothing — the code says it)* |\n| `re.compile(r\'^(?:[a-z]+-)*\\d{4}$\')` bare | `# matches kebab-prefixed 4-digit ids, e.g. "ord-handoff-0412"` |\n| `# acquire lock then update` | `# peers may write between read and update — hold the lock across both` |\n| `if delay > 30: # too long` | `# >30s exceeds the gateway timeout; fail fast instead of queueing` |\n\nA well-named constant often suffices on its own: add the why-comment only\nwhen the rationale is known and non-obvious — never invent one.\n\n## 4. Docs — spec and architecture only\n\n- The README / the project\'s agent context file carry: what the system is,\n  the architecture, how to run it. Plans and research live in committed plan\n  docs, cited by section — not scattered prose.\n- No doc that paraphrases code; no docstring that repeats the function name\n  (`def close_session(): """Closes the session."""` is noise).\n- When a doc that merely *describes* code behavior disagrees with the code,\n  the code is the truth — fix or delete the doc. When a **spec or\n  architecture doc** disagrees with the code, the code may be the bug:\n  raise the discrepancy, don\'t auto-resolve it in code\'s favor.\n\n## 5. Test-first definition of done\n\n**Strong default: write the meaningful long-term test first** — the test\nthat will still be guarding the behavior in a year — then implement until\ngreen. An untestable change description is a smell to resolve *before*\ncoding: rewrite the description until a test can be named, or escalate the\nambiguity to whoever owns the change. (Autonomous runner cards enforce\nthis; this default extends it to all work.)\n\n**Named exceptions** — where test-first is suspended, explicitly:\nspikes/experiments whose code will be thrown away (mark them as such; they\ndon\'t merge to mainline); pure throwaway scripts; and changes whose\nverification is observation rather than a unit test (docs, config, cosmetic\nUI) — state what observation replaces the test. If a spike graduates into\nshipped code, it gets its tests then — graduation is the gate.\n\nDiscipline around the tests themselves:\n\n- Scope test runs to what changed while iterating; run the full suite\n  before commit/merge.\n- Watch shared-fixture mutation (deep-copy before mutating shared\n  maps/slices), and run the full package before declaring green — a green\n  test that poisons its neighbor is worse than a red one.\n- Per-test timeouts: a silent hang eats budgets and hides the failure.\n- Test the real seam: projects carry a deployment smoke whose definition of\n  done is "fresh clone → README → working system".\n\n## 6. Change discipline — small, reversible, in-idiom\n\n- Small reversible changes; one concern per change. Fixes ride branches,\n  not mainline.\n- **Never restructure working code for style or consistency.** Clarity\n  improvements are renames, extracted constants, one-line comments on\n  tricky sections — not rewrites. During a bug fix the fix-plus-test\n  boundary wins even over those: a bug fix\'s diff is the fix and its test,\n  nothing else; clarity improvements and cleanup observations get\n  *mentioned* for a separate change, never bundled.\n- Match the surrounding code\'s idiom, comment density, and naming — a diff\n  readable as "more of the same file" reviews faster and reverts cleaner.\n\n## 7. Project overrides — the definition wins, out loud\n\nBefore writing code on any project, read its **board definition**\n(`get_definition` — `coding_standards`, `constraints`, `decisions`) and the\nproject\'s own agent context file. Their conventions **extend or\nspecialize** these defaults — a public SDK may mandate full docstrings on\nits public surface; a regulated codebase may mandate audit comments; both\nwin over sections 3–4 *for the surface they govern*.\n\nTwo rules make overrides safe:\n\n- **Apply overrides explicitly, not silently** — say "the project\'s\n  convention overrides the house default here" in the response, and in the\n  PR/commit description when the divergence lands in code (the override\'s\n  *why* is already durable in the board definition — that\'s what the next\n  session reads).\n- **Surface contradictions, never absorb them.** If a project convention\n  *contradicts* (not extends) a principle here in a way that seems\n  unintentional, or two project sources disagree with each other, raise it\n  to a human — silently picking a side buries a decision someone thought\n  was settled.\n\nWhen the user answers a convention question mid-session, record it in the\nboard definition (`update_definition`) so it survives the session.\n',
